# Fix determinism at the source, never by widening tolerance

<!-- core -->
When a test is flaky, fix the nondeterminism where it lives, then prove the fix reaches the code
under test. Pin the identity that varies, make the reset act, install a deterministic clock —
whichever mechanism the flake actually runs on. **Never make a flaky test pass by widening a
tolerance** — a pixel threshold, a timeout, a retry count, a sleep. A wider tolerance still passes
while the defect is present: it is the bug wearing a green badge, and the next flake is already on
its way.

Widen a tolerance only when the fuzziness is the requirement itself — the specification asks for an
approximate match and the tolerance states how approximate. A race, a hidden ordering or an
uninitialised value is never that.

**Prove the seam holds.** A determinism fix is done when the mechanism is shown to reach the code
under test, not when one green run follows it — state what the pin, the reset or the installed
clock replaces, and show the test failing with the mechanism removed.
<!-- /core -->

## Read the flake before touching anything

Run the test until you have seen it fail, and name what actually varied — time, identity, ordering,
leftover state, a live network, machine load. A flake you have not reproduced is a guess, and a
guess routes straight to the tolerance.

## Fix at the source

- **Pin identity.** When the assertion matches on something that varies run to run — a generated
  id, a timestamp, a random seed — pin it to a fixed value at the boundary the test controls, and
  assert against the pinned value.
- **Make the reset act.** A reset that exists but never fires is worse than none: it reads as
  coverage while the leftover state it was written for keeps leaking. Repair the mechanism so it
  actually acts, in the fixture, where every case passes through it.
- **Install a deterministic clock.** Freeze or install time at the boundary — Playwright's
  `page.clock.install()` for a page, the language's frozen-clock helper for a runtime — and assert
  against instants you named, never against the wall clock.

## Prove the seam holds

Break the mechanism on purpose — unpin the identity, disable the reset, unfreeze the clock — and
watch the test fail; restore it and watch the test pass. A pin that does not reach the code under
test proves nothing: a clock installed into the page but not into the wasm runtime it drives leaves
the original nondeterminism in place behind a green run. The failure you can produce on demand is
the evidence the fix is live; one green run after the fix is evidence of nothing.

## When widening is legitimate

Only when approximation is the requirement: the specification says the match is approximate and
the tolerance states how approximate. Name it in the assertion's own terms and move on. Never
widen to absorb a race, a hidden ordering, an uninitialised value, or a slow machine — fix the
cause instead, and if the cause is genuinely outside the test's reach, say so in the change
summary rather than quietly raising the threshold.

## Reporting

A change that touches flakiness says which mechanism it pinned, reset or installed and how the
seam was proven. A change that only widened a tolerance states why the source could not be fixed —
that sentence is the review target.

Applies to any automated test in any project — unit, integration, UI, visual snapshot, load — and
to the fixes written for them.
